# perfevent PMDA help file in ASCII format
#

@ perfevent.version The version number of the pmda.
@ perfevent.active The number of active counters.

@ perfevent.control.enabled Master switch for all of the hardware counters
Store one to enable the configured hardware counters, zero to disable them.

The counters only collect when this is one AND no perfalloc(1) read lock is
held on the perfevent lock file.  An external lock always wins, so storing one
while a lock is held records the request but leaves the counters off until the
lock is released; perfevent.control.lock_held reports whether that is the case.

While the counters are disabled their values stay frozen at the last reading
rather than being reported as errors, and perfevent.active reads zero.  This
is the same behaviour as an external perfalloc(1) lock.  Defaults to one.

@ perfevent.control.lock_held Whether an external perfalloc(1) lock is held
One when another process holds a read lock on the perfevent lock file, in
which case the hardware counters are disabled whatever perfevent.control.enabled
says.  Zero otherwise.  Read-only.

The lock file is polled every 100 milliseconds, so this can lag a perfalloc(1)
invocation by that much.

